        "Subsection 4.5.2 — Primary Autopsy: The Chad-Cameroon Pipeline and Petroleum Revenue Law No. 001/PR/99 (1999 to 2008). Few modern multilateral arrangements constructed a more elaborate financial waterfall on paper than the Chad-Cameroon Petroleum Development and Pipeline Project. Seeking financing to extract crude reserves in southern Chad's Doba basin and transport them via a 1,070-kilometre buried pipeline across Cameroon to the offshore Kribi export terminal—a venture representing approximately 4.2 billion dollars in total public-private investment backed by the World Bank Group alongside an ExxonMobil-led consortium—the Republic of Chad promulgated Law No. 001/PR/99 on Petroleum Revenue Management in January 1999 as a statutory prerequisite for World Bank board approval in June 2000.",
