        "IEG Report No. 50315 documented an additional operational flaw directly analogous to Vinculum's verification ledger checks: even before the statutory repeal of late 2005, Chad's joint oversight board (CCSRP) struggled with severe capacity, delayed audit data, and asymmetric procurement information inside N'Djamena ministries while conflict spilled across the eastern Darfur frontier. Once crude prices climbed well above initial 1999 project forecasts, Doba royalty inflows outstripped the leverage of outstanding concessional IBRD and IDA debt, allowing Chad to prepay roughly 65.7 million dollars from booming oil surpluses in September 2008 and exit all Bank covenants cleanly. The structural lesson reaches straight into treaty engineering: whenever escrow discipline depends upon creditor loan leverage rather than irrevocable source-captured trust partitions tied to continuous local bond health, windfall revenues or domestic security shocks flip negotiation leverage overnight and leave the intergenerational fund unguarded.",
