        "Subsection 4.5.3 — Comparative External-Custody Precedents: Algiers Accords Security Account (1981) and UNCC Oil Escrow (1991 to 2022). Two contrasting international precedents show how irrevocable third-party escrow design defeats unilateral amendment even across intense geopolitical hostility. First, in resolving the 1979–1981 hostage crisis under the General Declaration of the Algiers Accords (19 January 1981), Paragraph 7 established an interest-bearing Security Account held externally in the name of the Central Bank of Algeria as escrow agent at De Nederlandsche Bank's NV Settlement Bank in Amsterdam. Seeded initially with one billion dollars out of unfrozen Iranian assets, the Security Account carried a strict automatic replenishment covenant compelling Iran to deposit fresh funds whenever its balance dropped below five hundred million dollars—capital dedicated exclusively to paying binding monetary awards issued by the Iran–United States Claims Tribunal in The Hague. Neither claimant nor respondent could pass a domestic statute emptying Amsterdam's ledger or shifting disbursement priority, guaranteeing continuous satisfaction of adjudicated claims across four decades regardless of bilateral rupture.",
        "Second, following the 1991 Gulf ceasefire, the United Nations Security Council created the United Nations Compensation Commission (UNCC) Compensation Fund under Resolution 687 (1991), funded by an automated revenue waterfall under Resolution 705 (15 August 1991) and subsequent oil-for-food frameworks (Resolutions 706, 712, and later 1330). Under that mechanism, international buyers purchasing Iraqi petroleum remitted payments directly into a UN-administered escrow account before export receipts reached Baghdad; a statutory percentage—initially thirty per cent under Resolution 705, later adjusted to twenty-five per cent and eventually five per cent under Resolution 1483—was deducted automatically at source inside the trustee waterfall to settle verified war-reparation claims, humanitarian supply purchases, and administrative escrow obligations in strictly ranked categories. Between 1994 and its final payout closing operations in January 2022, the UNCC escrow verified and disbursed over fifty-two billion dollars precisely because buyers paid an external clearing trustee under unbreakable partition percentages rather than trusting voluntary post-sale domestic transfers.",
        "Notice how comparing Chad-Cameroon against Algiers and the UNCC illuminates one decisive institutional asymmetry: escrow conservation requires separating *source capture*, *rule custody*, and *disbursement certification* across distinct institutional actors. Chad's 1999 framework achieved offshore source capture (oil majors wired royalties straight into Citibank London) and multi-stakeholder certification through the CCSRP, yet left rule custody inside Chad's national legislature—the single actor subject to budget pressure. By contrast, Algiers lodged capital with De Nederlandsche Bank under a multilateral executive declaration whose replenishment floor bound Tehran automatically until certified Hague awards cleared, while UNCC locked buyer remittances and percentage deductions inside binding Chapter VII Security Council resolutions immune to unilateral treasury amendment. Even when the Chad-Cameroon interim memorandum of mid-2006 briefly patched sector earmarks after the January 2006 freeze, it functioned strictly as bilateral crisis bargaining rather than an objective bus gate, ending altogether when high 2008 crude prices let Chad prepay World Bank principal and close external supervision cleanly.",
