            "These categories describe different states. A clawback reserve is retained under the schedule's proportional rule and may be available under the conditions specified by the instrument. A performance bond is a separate retained amount with its own defined purpose and release condition. A freeze is an emergency state that prevents disbursement even if milestones have otherwise been met. An unmet tranche has not become releasable because its condition is not satisfied. Combining all four into a single label such as 'held funds' erases distinctions that matter for both accounting and remedy.",
            "The existing worked case with the first three tranches met and Final Cession pending illustrates the arithmetic. Gross met tranches total 80. The default clawback reserve retains 8, and the performance bond retains 5, so the released share is 67. The remaining 20 belongs to the unmet final tranche. Those amounts account for the entire pledge, and each has a different explanation. In particular, the five-point performance bond is not an additional penalty invented after the fact; it is a specified reserve in the default schedule.",
            "When the final condition is satisfied, the reserves are discharged under the stated schedule, and the release can reach the full pledge if no freeze applies. If critical rupture does apply, the freeze controls release even when the milestone checklist is green. This is a distinction between satisfaction of performance conditions and present permission to disburse. A team should report both rather than infer one from the other.",
            "The conservation view also provides an audit question: can an independent reviewer reconcile the amount promised, the tranche shares, the amounts certified, the reserve balances, the frozen amount, and the released amount? If the answer is no, the schedule may be incomplete, the record may be stale, or an accounting category may have been omitted. Conservation does not tell the reviewer whether the policy is wise; it tells the reviewer whether the value has been accounted for under the defined partition.",
            "Custom schedules change the timing and distribution of conditional exposure, but the conservation requirement remains. A balanced schedule can still be badly designed if it concentrates too much value in the earliest condition or places too little behind the conditions that protect long-term custody. Conversely, an unpopular schedule is not mathematically invalid merely because it differs from the default, provided it meets the invariant and any applicable legal requirements. Accounting validity and policy quality are separate judgments."
