            "The waterfall is ordered, so a later condition cannot be treated as an independent route around an earlier failed gate. This is a sequential contract design: each stage assumes that the prior stage has been cleared, and later tranches remain inaccessible until the chain reaches them. The consequence is that the earliest unmet condition can dominate the immediate payout even where later criteria appear strong.",
            "That property is different from a weighted score. A weighted score may allow a strong result on one dimension to offset a weak result on another. An ordered waterfall instead defines a path: first mobilise verified oversight, then establish the specified communal condition, then meet the operational and institutional condition, and finally satisfy the generational and survival requirement. The chain expresses a theory about sequencing, not just relative importance.",
