            "A release condition should be defined in terms that an independent reviewer can apply to evidence. Terms such as 'substantial progress' or 'satisfactory cooperation' may be politically useful during negotiation, but they leave the certifier with broad discretion unless the agreement supplies criteria, process, and reasons. A clearer condition identifies what has to happen, what record demonstrates it, who may inspect that record, and how a disputed finding is reviewed.",
            "Overly rigid drafting creates the opposite danger. If a condition can be satisfied only by a particular document, institution, or timetable, an unforeseen but substantively equivalent means of compliance may be excluded. Flexibility can be designed through a defined equivalence procedure rather than an open-ended waiver. The key is that any permitted alternative must be authorised by the instrument and applied transparently.",
